== CLAUDE.md managed block:
<!-- riskrails:start -->
# RiskRails (Pro plan)

Rules for every repository; everything else loads on demand. `~/.claude/riskrails/routing.md` has the model ladder, the context-guard thresholds and the launcher; a project's `.ai/AGENTS.md` routes to its policies.

- With `.ai/`: read `.ai/AGENTS.md` first, run every change through `/rr-build <request>`, treat `.ai/policies/` as binding; `/rr-setup --update` after a plugin reinstall. Without `.ai/`: `/rr-setup` first.
- Production behaviour is the source of truth: document problems outside the task, do not fix them. Never mix a refactoring with a feature change.
- Each step names the files it may touch; an edit outside them is refused — answer `SCOPE_CHANGE_REQUIRED`, do not widen the step.
- No agent merges or deploys. Commit, push and approval run only as the command the human picked in the dialog, as the harness recorded — verbatim, once — or the human runs them outside the agent. A guard's refusal is not a hint to reword.
- Verify before reporting done: the verification command once, to the end, every failure fixed as one batch, output shown.
- Context: `grep -n`, then read the hits; never `cat` logs, lockfiles or generated code; a file over ~4000 lines is read by a FAST reader (`Explore`, `log-reader`) that returns `file:line` excerpts, never the file. Deterministic tools first. `/clear` between tasks. `/rr-status --cost` shows where tokens went.
- Models are tiers, not names: runners and verbatim extraction on FAST at `low`, readers that judge on BALANCED; the session implements; STRONG and EXPERT only on a named trigger, one agent at a time, never a fleet on the top tier; a failed thinking task is never retried on a cheaper model.
- On compaction keep decisions and their reasons, rejected options, exact paths and names, failed attempts with the error text, open questions and the latest instruction verbatim; trust `.ai/state/handoff.md` and the guard's "Session state before compaction" over the summary.
<!-- riskrails:end -->
== routing.md (on demand): <HOME>/.claude/riskrails/routing.md
# Model routing (Pro plan)

Facts are collected cheaply; thinking is paid for. Session model Opus 5 in plan mode, Sonnet 5 when executing (opusplan), fallback Sonnet 5, default effort `medium`.

| Tier | Model / effort | Used for |
|---|---|---|
| FAST | `haiku` / `low` | reading and running: file search, listings, counting, logs and test output, running a command and reporting it (`Explore`, `log-reader`) |
| BALANCED | `sonnet` / `low`–`medium` | discovery with judgement, the review in guarded mode (`ai-discovery`, `ai-reviewer` in guarded mode) |
| STRONG | `opus` / `high` | the review in full mode, adversarial and security review (`ai-reviewer`, with a `focus: security` brief when domain is 2) |
| EXPERT | `opus` / `high`, pinned — on opusplan an inherited model is Sonnet outside plan mode | design (`architect`), only when STRONG said it cannot settle the question |

- Session model is `opusplan`: Opus 5 in plan mode, Sonnet 5 when executing. Use plan mode for T3+ and for a T2 that spans several modules — that is where Opus is paid for. Fable is off this plan; never request it. `xhigh`/`max` are unavailable. STRONG and EXPERT both pin `model: opus` explicitly.
- Every agent definition and Workflow `agent()` call declares `model:` and `effort:`. Readers and runners never go above `low`. Forks inherit the session and take no `model`.
- Five or more parallel agents never run on `opus`. Escalate one task at a time on a stated trigger, never the whole fleet; never retry a failed thinking task on a cheaper model.
- Raise to `high` only for architecture, root-cause analysis and adversarial verification, and say that you are raising it.
- Planning, the weight, the test run and the release are the session's work, not an agent's: `/rr-plan`, `state.py weight`, `state.py verify`, `/rr-ship`.
- Implement in the main session. Delegate reading: logs and test output to `log-reader`, wide searches to `Explore` — both on `haiku`. Nothing below T4 runs on `opus`. The conclusion a human reads is written here, never delegated.
- Split code reading by how many hops it needs. A lookup — where X is, one route, service id, config key or template — goes to `Explore` on `haiku`. A trace of how the *installed* third-party code behaves (`vendor/`, `node_modules/`: decorators, compiler passes, service or template overrides, event subscribers) goes to a BALANCED reader at `low` — `ai-discovery`, or `Explore` spawned with `model: sonnet` where there is no `.ai/` — because a FAST reader stops at the first plausible match, and there a wrong answer reads like a right one.

# Context hygiene

- Search with `grep -n` and read the hits. No unbounded `Read` over ~4000 lines — a hook refuses it, and a `limit` larger than that budget is refused too. Never `cat` logs, lockfiles, migrations or generated code.
- A file too large to open is read by the cheapest model, never here: send `Explore` (code) or `log-reader` (logs, test output) and take back only the relevant excerpt with `file:line` — never the file.
- Tools and MCP servers are context too, in every turn, used or not. Default off: `enableAllProjectMcpServers: false`, a project enables only what nearly every task needs, a task names anything extra in one line and turns it off again, and deferred tools are loaded in one batched call. See `.ai/policies/tooling.md`.
- One subagent returning twenty lines beats five tool calls whose output stays in context all session.
- Deterministic tools first: `rg`, `git`, `jq`, the framework CLI, the test runner.
- `/clear` between tasks, and after every task that ran a review. Compaction near 167 000 tokens is a summary of the old task, not a clean start; until then every turn re-reads the whole history.
- `context-guard` measures the context on every prompt: a one-line warning from 133k tokens, and the prompt is never held back (only `--context economy` sets a block threshold). When its note says the prompt starts a different task, suggest `/clear` before doing the work. Before a compaction it saves the edited files, the latest instructions verbatim, the todo list and the git state, and hands them back afterwards as "Session state before compaction": trust that block over the summary where they differ, and re-read a file before editing it rather than relying on what the summary says it contains.
- Run tests once, to the end, and fix every failure as one batch. Never one failure, one fix, one run.
- `/rr-status --cost` shows where the tokens went, at zero model cost; `--provider both` puts Codex next to Claude Code.

# Guards

- `ai-git-guard` runs in every repository: no force push, history rewrite, push or merge to a protected branch, `--no-verify`, staged secret or production deploy. `ai-path-guard` and `ai-scope-guard` arm only where `.ai/` exists. No agent merges or deploys; commit, push and approval run only as the command the human picked in the dialog, as the harness recorded — verbatim, once — or the human runs them outside the agent.
- `context-guard` measures the context on every prompt and holds a prompt back once at the block threshold above, when there is one; its "Session state before compaction" block and `.ai/state/handoff.md` are what a resumed session trusts over the summary.

- Shared `.ai/` files — policies, agent contracts, workflows, templates — are installed once in `<HOME>/.claude/riskrails/ai/`. A project's own regular file at the same path under `.ai/` overrides it; `<HOME>/.claude/riskrails/ai-path <path>` prints the one to read.
